LeetCode 542 — 01 Matrix
Problem

Given an m x n binary matrix mat, return a matrix where:

Every 0 remains 0.
Every 1 is replaced by its distance from the nearest 0.
You can move only up, down, left, right.
Example
Input:
0 0 0
0 1 0
1 1 1

Output:
0 0 0
0 1 0
1 2 1

For example, the bottom-middle 1:

1 → 1 → 0

has distance 2.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: BFS From Every 1
Idea

For every 1:

Start BFS from that particular cell.
Search until we find a 0.
The first 0 found gives the shortest distance.
Repeat for every 1.
Example

For:

0 0 0
0 1 0
1 1 1

For the center 1:

      1
     / \
    0   0

Nearest 0 is at distance 1.

For bottom-middle:

0 0 0
0 1 0
1 1 1
    ↑

Nearest 0:

(2,1) → (1,1) → (1,2)

Distance = 2.

Java Code
class Solution {

    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        int[][] ans = new int[m][n];

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {
                    ans[i][j] = 0;
                } else {
                    ans[i][j] = bfs(mat, i, j);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    private int bfs(int[][] mat, int sr, int sc) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[m][n];

        q.add(new int[]{sr, sc});
        visited[sr][sc] = true;

        int distance = 0;

        int[] dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 1, 0, -1};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                int[] curr = q.poll();

                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];

                if (mat[r][c] == 0) {
                    return distance;
                }

                for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++) {

                    int nr = r + dr[j];
                    int nc = c + dc[j];

                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                        !visited[nr][nc]) {

                        visited[nr][nc] = true;
                        q.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }

            distance++;
        }

        return -1;
    }
}

Complexity

There can be m × n starting cells, and each BFS can visit m × n cells.

Time  = O((m × n)²)
Space = O(m × n)

❌ Too slow for large matrices.

Approach 2 — Brute Force Using DFS

We can also recursively search from every 1.

The idea is:

For every 1:
    recursively explore neighbors
    find nearest 0

However, plain DFS is not a good shortest-path algorithm.

DFS can go very deep in one direction before discovering a nearby 0.

For example:

1 1 1 1 0

A DFS might explore:

1 → 1 → 1 → 1 → 0

before checking another direction.

So we would need:

visited tracking
minimum distance
backtracking
potentially many repeated paths

This makes it much more complicated and inefficient than BFS.

Basic DFS Code

class Solution {

    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        int[][] ans = new int[m][n];

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {
                    ans[i][j] = 0;
                } else {
                    ans[i][j] = dfs(mat, i, j,
                                    new boolean[m][n]);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    private int dfs(
        int[][] mat,
        int r,
        int c,
        boolean[][] visited) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        if (r < 0 || r >= m ||
            c < 0 || c >= n ||
            visited[r][c]) {

            return Integer.MAX_VALUE / 2;
        }

        if (mat[r][c] == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        visited[r][c] = true;

        int up = dfs(mat, r - 1, c, visited);
        int down = dfs(mat, r + 1, c, visited);
        int left = dfs(mat, r, c - 1, visited);
        int right = dfs(mat, r, c + 1, visited);

        visited[r][c] = false;

        return 1 + Math.min(
            Math.min(up, down),
            Math.min(left, right)
        );
    }
}

Complexity

In the worst case, there can be a huge number of possible paths.

Time = Very large / exponential in the worst case
Space = O(m × n)

❌ Not recommended.

Important Lesson

For shortest distance in an unweighted grid:

DFS ❌
BFS ✅

Approach 3 — Dynamic Programming: Two-Pass Solution

There is another excellent O(m × n) approach.

Key Idea

For every 1, its shortest distance can come from any of four directions:

       Up
       ↑
Left ← 1 → Right
       ↓
      Down

We can calculate the answer in two passes.

Pass 1

Move:

Top → Bottom
Left → Right

At each cell, consider:

up
left
Pass 2

Move:

Bottom → Top
Right → Left

Consider:

down
right

Together, these cover all four directions.

Java Code
class Solution {

    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        int INF = m + n;

        int[][] ans = new int[m][n];

        // Initialize
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {
                    ans[i][j] = 0;
                } else {
                    ans[i][j] = INF;
                }
            }
        }

        // Pass 1: Top -> Bottom, Left -> Right
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (ans[i][j] != 0) {

                    if (i > 0) {
                        ans[i][j] = Math.min(
                            ans[i][j],
                            ans[i - 1][j] + 1
                        );
                    }

                    if (j > 0) {
                        ans[i][j] = Math.min(
                            ans[i][j],
                            ans[i][j - 1] + 1
                        );
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Pass 2: Bottom -> Top, Right -> Left
        for (int i = m - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {

                if (i < m - 1) {
                    ans[i][j] = Math.min(
                        ans[i][j],
                        ans[i + 1][j] + 1
                    );
                }

                if (j < n - 1) {
                    ans[i][j] = Math.min(
                        ans[i][j],
                        ans[i][j + 1] + 1
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

