Here this example already happens to be correct.

The second pass ensures cases where the shortest path must come from the bottom or right are also corrected.

Complexity
Time  = O(m × n)
Space = O(m × n)

This is optimal time complexity.

Approach 4 — Multi-Source BFS ⭐ Most Optimal / Best Pattern

This is the most important solution for this problem.

Key Observation

Instead of doing:

For every 1:
    Find nearest 0

reverse the problem.

Put all zeros into the queue initially.

0 0 0
0 1 0
1 1 1

Initial queue:

(0,0)
(0,1)
(0,2)
(1,0)
(1,2)

Every zero has distance:

0

Then BFS expands outward:

0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → ...

Because BFS explores by increasing distance, the first time a cell is visited, we know the shortest distance to the nearest zero.

This is called:

Multi-Source BFS

Your Code ⭐

Your code is a correct and optimal multi-source BFS solution.

class Pair {
    int row;
    int col;
    int step;

    public Pair(int row, int col, int step) {
        this.row = row;
        this.col = col;
        this.step = step;
    }
}

class Solution {

    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();

        int vis[][] = new int[m][n];
        int ans[][] = new int[m][n];

        // Put all 0s into the queue
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {

                    q.add(new Pair(i, j, 0));

                    vis[i][j] = 1;
                }
            }
        }

        int dr[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};
        int dc[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int r = q.peek().row;
            int c = q.peek().col;
            int s = q.peek().step;

            q.remove();

            ans[r][c] = s;

            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {

                int nr = r + dr[i];
                int nc = c + dc[i];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                    nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                    vis[nr][nc] == 0) {

                    vis[nr][nc] = 1;

                    q.add(new Pair(nr, nc, s + 1));
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

