Why Does Multi-Source BFS Guarantee the Minimum?

Suppose we have:

0 . . . 1

The zero starts at distance 0.

BFS visits:

0
↓
1
↓
2
↓
3
↓
4

So when the final cell is reached, it gets:

4

Now imagine there are two zeros:

0 . . . 0

Both start simultaneously:

0       0
↓       ↓
1       1
↓       ↓
2   2   2

The cell in the middle gets whichever distance reaches it first.

Therefore:

first visit = nearest zero
Why vis Is Important

Your code uses:

if (... && vis[nr][nc] == 0)

This means:

Only add a cell if it has never been visited.

Then:

vis[nr][nc] = 1;

marks it immediately.

This prevents the same cell from being inserted into the queue multiple times.

Direction Arrays

Your code:

int dr[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};
int dc[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0};

represents:

           (-1,0)
              ↑
              |
(0,-1) ←──── CELL ────→ (0,1)
              |
              ↓
           (1,0)

So we check:

Left
Down
Right
Up

Order doesn't matter.

Approach 5 — Multi-Source BFS Without Separate step in Queue

We can make the solution slightly cleaner.

Instead of storing:

Pair(row, col, step)

we can store only:

(row, col)

and process BFS level by level.

The queue size tells us which distance we're currently processing.

Java Code
class Solution {

    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        int[][] ans = new int[m][n];

        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();

        // -1 means unvisited
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            Arrays.fill(ans[i], -1);
        }

        // Add all zeros
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {

                    ans[i][j] = 0;

                    q.add(new int[]{i, j});
                }
            }
        }

        int[] dr = {0, 1, 0, -1};
        int[] dc = {-1, 0, 1, 0};

        int distance = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            for (int k = 0; k < size; k++) {

                int[] curr = q.poll();

                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];

                for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {

                    int nr = r + dr[i];
                    int nc = c + dc[i];

                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                        ans[nr][nc] == -1) {

                        ans[nr][nc] = distance + 1;

                        q.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }

            distance++;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Complexity
Time  = O(m × n)
Space = O(m × n)
Approach Comparison
#	Approach	Time	Space	Recommended
1	BFS from every 1	O((mn)²)	O(mn)	❌
2	DFS from every 1	Very large	O(mn)	❌
3	Two-Pass DP	O(mn)	O(mn)	✅
4	Multi-Source BFS	O(mn)	O(mn)	⭐
5	Multi-Source BFS level-by-level	O(mn)	O(mn)	⭐
Why Your Solution Is Optimal

Your solution does:

1. Find all zeros
       ↓
2. Put all zeros in queue
       ↓
3. BFS simultaneously
       ↓
4. First visit = shortest distance

Every cell is:

visited once
↓
inserted once
↓
removed once
↓
4 neighbors checked

Therefore:

Time = O(m × n)

You cannot do better asymptotically because the matrix contains m × n cells and we need to determine the answer for each one.

Important Pattern ⭐ — Multi-Source BFS

This is the main concept you should remember from 542. 01 Matrix.

Whenever a problem says:

Find the shortest distance from every cell to the nearest one of multiple starting points.

Think:

Multiple Sources
       ↓
Put ALL sources in Queue
       ↓
BFS
       ↓
Level = Distance
       ↓
First Visit = Minimum Distance
